V. Quick Sort:
Definition
Quick Sort is a divide-and-conquer sorting algorithm that selects an element as a pivot and partitions the array around the pivot.
After partitioning, elements smaller than the pivot are placed on one side and elements greater than the pivot are placed on the other side.
The same process is then recursively applied to the left and right subarrays until the entire array becomes sorted.
Time Complexity
Best Case: O(n log n)
Average Case: O(n log n)
Worst Case: O(n²)
Auxiliary Space
Quick Sort uses O(log n) auxiliary space on average due to the recursive function calls.
In the worst case, when the partitions are highly unbalanced, the recursion depth can become O(n).
Therefore:
Average Auxiliary Space: O(log n)
Worst-Case Auxiliary Space: O(n)
Conclusion
Quick Sort is an efficient divide-and-conquer sorting algorithm that works by selecting a pivot and partitioning the array around it.
It has an average time complexity of O(n log n), making it efficient for large datasets.
However, its worst-case time complexity can become O(n²) when the partitions are highly unbalanced.
Quick Sort is widely used because of its good average performance and in-place partitioning approach.
 
